WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.360
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.221
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.521 --> 00:00:17.330
Unit thirteen: Gender Equality. Today you will contrast ideas with whereas, nevertheless and even so.

4
00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.247
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.305
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.383
Gender pay gap.

7
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:31.473
Gender pay gap means the average difference in pay between women and men.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.652
The gender pay gap narrowed slightly this year.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.076
Your turn. Say: Gender pay gap.

10
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:42.306
Gender pay gap.

11
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.632
Workplace equality.

12
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:49.509
Workplace equality means equal treatment of all employees at work.

13
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:53.277
Workplace equality requires fair hiring and promotion.

14
00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:56.977
Your turn. Say: Workplace equality.

15
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:01.612
Workplace equality.

16
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.595
Representation.

17
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:08.661
Representation means the presence of a group among those who make decisions.

18
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.341
Women's representation in parliament reached a record high.

19
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.974
Your turn. Say: Representation.

20
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:20.461
Representation.

21
00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:22.926
Gender-based discrimination.

22
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:28.192
Gender-based discrimination means unfair treatment because of a person's gender.

23
00:01:28.783 --> 00:01:31.944
Gender-based discrimination is illegal in the workplace.

24
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.806
Your turn. Say: Gender-based discrimination.

25
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:40.446
Gender-based discrimination.

26
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:42.614
Equal opportunity.

27
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:47.334
Equal opportunity means the same chance for everyone to succeed.

28
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.227
Equal opportunity policies protect job applicants.

29
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:54.864
Your turn. Say: Equal opportunity.

30
00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.038
Equal opportunity.

31
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:01.051
Women's rights.

32
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.988
Women's rights means the legal and social rights of women and girls.

33
00:02:06.583 --> 00:02:09.152
Women's rights campaigners won the right to vote.

34
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.774
Your turn. Say: Women's rights.

35
00:02:15.633 --> 00:02:17.064
Women's rights.

36
00:02:17.817 --> 00:02:19.062
Glass ceiling.

37
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.766
Glass ceiling means an invisible barrier that stops people reaching top jobs.

38
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.534
She broke the glass ceiling and became chief executive.

39
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.116
Your turn. Say: Glass ceiling.

40
00:02:34.967 --> 00:02:36.246
Glass ceiling.

41
00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:38.356
Policy reform.

42
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.998
Policy reform means an official change to rules to make them fairer.

43
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:46.930
Policy reform introduced paid leave for both parents.

44
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:50.610
Your turn. Say: Policy reform.

45
00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:54.793
Policy reform.

46
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.111
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:07.966
Visal, a report says 'Women have gained representation in parliament; nevertheless, a glass ceiling remains.' What does 'nevertheless' do?

48
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:20.143
It shows contrast with the previous statement. Nevertheless, nonetheless and even so are used at the start of a new sentence or after a semicolon, with a comma.

49
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.820
How is 'whereas' different?

50
00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:39.653
Whereas and whilst join two clauses in one sentence to compare: Women in some sectors earn more, whereas the gender pay gap is wider in others. They are formal and common in reports.

51
00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:42.935
Which punctuation should I remember?

52
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:55.201
Use a comma before whereas, a semicolon before nevertheless, and a comma after it. Equal opportunity laws exist; nonetheless, gender-based discrimination persists.

53
00:03:56.367 --> 00:03:58.018
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:58.883 --> 00:04:03.147
Grammar focus: Contrast: whereas and nevertheless and even so.

55
00:04:03.447 --> 00:04:04.796
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:05.483 --> 00:04:10.501
Equal opportunity laws exist; nevertheless, gender-based discrimination persists.

57
00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.740
The gap is narrow in teaching, whereas it is wide in finance.

58
00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.356
Progress has been slow; even so, women's rights have advanced.

59
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:28.046
Use whereas / whilst inside a sentence with a comma; use nevertheless / nonetheless / even so after a semicolon or full stop, followed by a comma.

60
00:04:28.783 --> 00:04:34.512
Be careful! Don't say: Laws exist, nevertheless discrimination persists.

61
00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:41.171
Say: Laws exist; nevertheless, discrimination persists.

62
00:04:41.471 --> 00:04:44.098
Use a semicolon before and a comma after.

63
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.576
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:48.367 --> 00:04:50.192
The Distance Still to Travel.

65
00:04:50.492 --> 00:04:53.664
Many countries have introduced laws for workplace equality.

66
00:04:54.667 --> 00:05:00.416
Representation in politics has improved; nevertheless, women remain a minority in most parliaments.

67
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.547
The gender pay gap is small in some professions, whereas it is wide in others.

68
00:05:06.533 --> 00:05:14.164
A glass ceiling still blocks talented women from top jobs; even so, women's rights movements keep pushing for equal opportunity.

69
00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:18.757
Policy reform therefore remains necessary, not optional.

70
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:23.741
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.583 --> 00:05:27.500
The gap is small here, blank it is large elsewhere.

72
00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.546
A, whereas. B, nevertheless. C, therefore.

73
00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.216
The answer is A: whereas.

74
00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.489
Choose the correct punctuation.

75
00:05:40.789 --> 00:05:51.559
A, Laws exist, nevertheless discrimination persists.. B, Laws exist; nevertheless, discrimination persists.. C, Laws exist nevertheless; discrimination persists..

76
00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.828
The answer is B: Laws exist; nevertheless, discrimination persists..

77
00:06:00.983 --> 00:06:03.993
An invisible barrier to promotion is a glass blank.

78
00:06:04.293 --> 00:06:07.261
A, ceiling. B, door. C, wall.

79
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.660
The answer is A: ceiling.

80
00:06:13.817 --> 00:06:15.758
Which linker shows contrast?

81
00:06:16.058 --> 00:06:20.153
A, as a result. B, consequently. C, even so.

82
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.990
The answer is C: even so.

83
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.974
According to the reading, policy reform remains…

84
00:06:30.274 --> 00:06:34.090
A, optional. B, necessary. C, finished.

85
00:06:37.633 --> 00:06:39.784
The answer is B: necessary.

86
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.314
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.100
What is one concrete policy that could help close the gender pay gap?

88
00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.347
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:59.647 --> 00:07:28.297
One effective policy is mandatory pay transparency, which requires companies to publish salary ranges and report pay differences between women and men. When employers must explain gaps, unfair patterns become visible and easier to correct. This can be combined with affordable childcare and equal parental leave, so that women are not forced to leave work more often than men.

90
00:07:28.597 --> 00:07:30.295
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:36.518
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and contrast: whereas and nevertheless and even so.

92
00:07:36.818 --> 00:07:42.572
Today's value is equality. Treat everyone fairly. We all deserve the same chances.

93
00:07:42.872 --> 00:07:45.339
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:48.698
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

